  people living in the central part of  britain in the year 400 were what we  would call romanized britons this means  a large portion of the native population  that was mostly originally culturally  celtic so they're the ancestors of  people we think of today as the irish  the welsh the scots  these people over time had come to think  of themselves as romans with all the  rights and privileges of any citizen of  the empire  rome's decline has begun nearly two  centuries earlier  around the year 235 ce the emperor  severus alexander was murdered in a  mutiny led by one of his army commanders  from this moment forward the empire  becomes increasingly unstable  invading tribes of goths visigoths huns  and vandals move into the power vacuum  fighting one another and threatening the  imperial authorities  still it's important to understand that  the goths and romans have never been  implacable enemies they've had close  links since the middle of the third  century particularly in  trade in the half century from  235 to 284  the roman senate will ratify no fewer  than 25 emperors  and that's not counting the several  pretenders who create fiefdoms and lay  claim to the imperial purple  the romans who live in the capital  become increasingly anxious to protect  themselves from the barbarian hordes  that are sweeping into italy from the  north  the able emperor diocletian restored  some semblance of order late in the  third century dividing what had become  an ungovernable empire into eastern and  western halves each with its own  leadership  then civil war erupted and constantine  fought his way to power  constantine claimed to have had a vision  the night before the crucial battle of  the milvian bridge in which he saw  himself raising a cross as his symbol  his success in the battle encouraged him  to decree in the year 313 of the common  era that christianity would henceforth  be tolerated throughout the empire  12 years later in 325 he invited all of  the leading christian thinkers to  assemble at the council of nicaea which  resolved critical disputes over  christian beliefs  in 330 constantine made the old greek  city of byzantium the capital of the  eastern empire and it was later renamed  constantinople  but the endgame of roman civilization at  least in its classic form was fast  approaching  various german tribes have been  increasingly drawn into the power vacuum  created by the crisis of the 3rd century  the roman army recruited many germans  into its ranks and allowed others to  cross the borders and settle on roman  land although many of these communities  were mistreated by corrupt roman  officials  german restlessness was exacerbated by  the arrival of the militarized huns from  central asia  in keeping with the popular perception  this was indeed a period when the  peoples commonly referred to as  barbarians invaded  or some would say  migrated into the roman empire in  hitherto unprecedented numbers  there were also new bands of barbarians  who appeared on the scene and posed a  greater threat to rome than it had  previously experienced  who were these barbarians the term  barbarian was originally a greek one  simply meaning  those who could not speak greek  but it eventually became a pejorative  label  used by the greeks and romans to denote  almost any group that they regarded as  less civilized than themselves  during the republic and early empire the  nations labeled as barbarians who fought  against rome in gaul germany spain  britain and the balkans were not one  unified society or civilization  but were instead dozens of separate  tribes  each with its own specific culture  traditions language and religious  practices  during the early empire clashes and  sometimes full-scale wars against  innumerable barbarian nations along the  rhine and danube frontiers had been a  constant preoccupation  these conflicts had included great  victories for rome but there had been  major disasters as well  nevertheless the existence of the empire  itself  had rarely if ever been threatened  and no hostile barbarian had set foot in  the city of rome for eight centuries  it's important to understand that the  barbarians are not intent on destroying  the empire  rather they want accommodation within it  not infrequently barbarian tribes and  leaders would be recruited by one  faction or another  given roman titles and privileges and  then employed in civil wars  there was also a growing tendency for  the eastern and western halves of the  empire to  turn inwards and focus on their own  problems failing to cooperate when  common threats appeared and refusing to  support one another  all of this was complicated even further  by continued external pressure from  barbarian tribes on the frontiers  and economic crises within the empire  in the early 400s one of the most  important barbarian warlords who became  enmeshed in roman politics was a  visigoth named alaric  at various points in his career he had  served in the roman army fought on  theodosius's side against arbogast at  the battle of the frigates river  turned against the romans and looted a  number of major cities  been bought off by the romans with huge  cash bribes been appointed the general  in charge of all roman forces in the  lyricum  fought with steliko invaded italy and  threatened to attack rome  been bought off yet again with money and  titles again threatened to invade and  then actually block hated rome  finally in 4 10 a.d alaric and a band of  visigoths marched down into italy  captured rome  and for three days  looted the capital  although the damage to the city was not  severe and alaric and the visigoths soon  departed with their plunder the  psychological effect of this blow was  immense  for the first time in over 800 years a  foreign enemy had occupied the  traditional capital of the empire  the city of rome was  invested with profound symbolic  significance for the romans  and their failure to protect it made  plain the reduced state of roman power  as nothing else could have  the sack of rome plainly advertised just  how weak the empire or at least the  western half of it had become  a message that other barbarian tribes  would be quick to take note of  as a result of this the result of this  sacking  rome called all the legions back to the  center of the empire  now  take a second and imagine  what would happen if almost overnight  all your peacekeeping and your military  personnel disappeared and a population  used to comfort and order  and dare i say civilized living found  itself with no security infrastructure  whatsoever  those left behind were farmers crafts  people  members of religious orders in other  words they were people who were  completely unprepared for what happened  next  and what happened next is that the picks  from scotland and the scots from ireland  and all kinds of germanic groups from  the continent recognize that hey  britain is ripe for raiding  and raid it  they did  among those attacking groups were  raiders from tribes that hailed from  non-romanized northern europe and this  is the region that the romans had often  referred to as  germania these groups were culturally  similar to what we tend to think of when  we think of our sort of stereotypical  viking  they were pagan  warlike they were excellent seafaring  peoples and they worshipped gods similar  to those in the norse pantheon like odin  they included groups  like the angles the saxons the jutes the  phrygians danes swedes  norse and a whole bunch of others that  it would take too long to go into here  germans now settled all over western  europe the visigoths in spain the  burgundians in the rhone valley the  francs widely across gaul and the angles  saxons and jutes in britain  the huns meanwhile led by the  charismatic attila were finally defeated  by a combined roman and visigoth army  near toy and their confederation  completely disintegrated after attila  died of a hemorrhage in 